Subject: an xattr walk checks its bounds before it dereferences, not after

ext4_xattr_is_ibody_valid() read three things before it had established
that any of them were inside the inode buffer:

  * the header's magic number. EXT4_XATTR_IHDR places the header at
    inode + 128 + i_extra_isize, and i_extra_isize comes off the medium,
    so a corrupt value puts the header past the end of the inode.
  * min_offs, computed as end - base, which underflows into an enormous
    size_t when base is already past end.
  * the first entry's four bytes, through EXT4_XATTR_IS_LAST_ENTRY, in
    the loop's own condition -- before any bound was checked.

The loop's guard against walking off the end lived inside its body, after
the entry's fields had already been read, so it protected the entry after
next and not the one in hand.

    ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow
      ext4_xattr_is_ibody_valid ext4_xattr.c:591
      ext4_xattr_list <- ext4b_listxattr

Found by fuzzing, in a thirty-second campaign. Delta-minimized to ONE
byte: offset 0x828 of the seed, which is block 2 byte 40 -- group
descriptor 1's bg_inode_table_lo. 0x27 becomes 0x00, the group's inode
table moves 39 blocks earlier into the metadata region, and the inode
read from there is whatever the bitmaps happen to contain.

Both walks now check bounds before dereferencing: four bytes to ask
whether an entry is the terminator, the whole struct before reading its
fields. The block walk's first entry is always in range -- it sits 32
bytes into a block -- but its loop had the same shape and now has the
same guard.

Fixture Tests/fixtures/hostile/0004-inode-table-moved-under-an-xattr-walk.img.gz.

--- a/src/ext4_xattr.c
+++ b/src/ext4_xattr.c
@@ -528,8 +528,20 @@
